{"task_id": "HumanEval/40", "solution": "def triples_sum_to_zero(l: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if any three distinct elements in the list l sum to zero.\n    Distinctness is based on the original indices of elements in the input list.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(l)\n    if n < 3:\n        return False\n\n    # Create a list of (value, original_index) pairs and sort it by value.\n    # This allows identifying distinct elements by their original indices.\n    indexed_numbers = sorted([(value, index) for index, value in enumerate(l)])\n\n    for i in range(n - 2):\n        # Fix the first element from the sorted list.\n        a_val, _ = indexed_numbers[i]\n        \n        # Use two pointers for the remaining elements.\n        left_ptr, right_ptr = i + 1, n - 1\n\n        while left_ptr < right_ptr:\n            b_val, _ = indexed_numbers[left_ptr]\n            c_val, _ = indexed_numbers[right_ptr]\n\n            current_sum = a_val + b_val + c_val\n\n            if current_sum == 0:\n                # A triple summing to zero is found using distinct elements by index.\n                return True\n            elif current_sum < 0:\n                # Sum is too small, move left_ptr to increase sum.\n                left_ptr += 1\n            else: # current_sum > 0\n                # Sum is too large, move right_ptr to decrease sum.\n                right_ptr -= 1\n                \n    return False"}
