{"task_id": "HumanEval/44", "solution": "def change_base(x: int, base: int):\n    \"\"\"Change numerical base of input number x to base.\n    return string representation after the conversion.\n    \"\"\"\n    if x == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n    digits = []\n    n = x\n    while n > 0:\n        digits.append(str(n % base))\n        n //= base\n    return \"\".join(reversed(digits))"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/44", "solution": "```python\ndef change_base(x: int, base: int):\n    \"\"\"Change numerical base of input number x to base.\n    Return string representation after the conversion.\n    \"\"\"\n    if x == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n\n    negative = x < 0\n    n_abs = abs(x)\n    \n    digits = []\n    while n_abs > 0:\n        n_abs, rem = divmod(n_abs, base)\n        digits.append(str(rem))\n\n    result_str = \"\".join(reversed(digits))\n\n    if negative:\n        result_str = \"-\" + result_str\n        \n    return result_str\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/43", "solution": "```python\n# src/pairsum/__init__.py\n\"\"\"\nThe pairsum package provides a utility to determine if a list of integers\ncontains two distinct elements that sum to zero.\nIt offers a programmatic API, CLI, and HTTP API for diverse usage.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport logging\nimport os\nfrom .config import Config\nfrom .repository import PairSumRepository\nfrom .service import PairSumService\nfrom .controllers import CLIController, LibraryController\nfrom .api import create_app as create_fastapi_app\n\n# Initialize default logging configuration\n_config = Config()\nlogging.basicConfig(level=getattr(logging, _config.log_level.upper(), logging.INFO),\n                    format='%(asctime)s - %(name)s - %(levelname)s - %(message)s')\n\n# --- Public API exports ---\n# For programmatic use\nrepository = PairSumRepository(_config.db_url)\nservice = PairSumService(repository)\ncheck_pair_sum_to_zero = LibraryController(service).check\n\n# For FastAPI app creation\ncreate_app = create_fastapi_app\n```\n\n```python\n# src/pairsum/config.py\nimport os\n\nclass Config:\n    \"\"\"\n    Configuration class for the pairsum application, loading settings\n    from environment variables or providing sensible defaults.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.db_url: str = os.getenv(\"PAIRSUM_DB_URL\", \"sqlite:///data/pairsum.db\")\n        self.log_level: str = os.getenv(\"PAIRSUM_LOG_LEVEL\", \"INFO\")\n\n# Global config instance\napp_config = Config()\n```\n\n```python\n# src/pairsum/models.py\nfrom typing import List, Optional\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\nclass PairSumRequest(BaseModel):\n    \"\"\"\n    DTO for requests containing a list of numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    numbers: List[int]\n\nclass PairSumResult(BaseModel):\n    \"\"\"\n    DTO for results, including the boolean outcome and original input.\n    \"\"\"\n    result: bool\n    input: List[int]\n\nclass QueryRecord(BaseModel):\n    \"\"\"\n    ORM-like model for persisting query history.\n    \"\"\"\n    id: Optional[int] = None\n    timestamp: datetime\n    input_json: str\n    result: bool\n\n    class Config:\n        json_encoders = {\n            datetime: lambda v: v.isoformat(),\n        }\n```\n\n```python\n# src/pairsum/exceptions.py\nclass ValidationError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"\n    Exception raised for invalid input data.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str):\n        self.message = message\n        super().__init__(self.message)\n\nclass PersistenceError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"\n    Exception raised for failures during data persistence operations.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str):\n        self.message = message\n        super().__init__(self.message)\n```\n\n```python\n# src/pairsum/utils.py\nimport json\nfrom typing import Any, List\n\ndef parse_number_list_arg(arg: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string argument into a list of integers.\n    Handles JSON array, comma-separated, or space-separated strings.\n    \"\"\"\n    arg = arg.strip()\n    if arg.startswith(\"[\") or arg.startswith(\"{\"): # Assume JSON array\n        try:\n            parsed = json.loads(arg)\n            if not isinstance(parsed, list):\n                raise ValueError(\"JSON input must be an array.\")\n            return [int(x) for x in parsed]\n        except (json.JSONDecodeError, ValueError) as e:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Invalid JSON array: {e}\")\n    \n    # Try comma-separated or space-separated\n    separator = \",\" if \",\" in arg else \" \"\n    parts = [p.strip() for p in arg.split(separator) if p.strip()]\n    \n    try:\n        return [int(p) for p in parts]\n    except ValueError as e:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Invalid number format in string: {e}\")\n\ndef to_json_safe(obj: Any) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts an object to a JSON string safely for persistence.\n    Handles datetime objects by converting to ISO format.\n    \"\"\"\n    def default_serializer(o):\n        if isinstance(o, datetime):\n            return o.isoformat()\n        raise TypeError(f\"Object of type {o.__class__.__name__} is not JSON serializable\")\n    return json.dumps(obj, default=default_serializer)\n```\n\n```python\n# src/pairsum/validators.py\nimport json\nfrom typing import Any, List\nfrom .exceptions import ValidationError\nfrom .utils import parse_number_list_arg # Re-use utility parser\n\nclass PairSumValidator:\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides static methods for robustly parsing and validating input lists of numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def validate_numbers(input_value: Any) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validates an input value to ensure it represents a list of integers.\n        Accepts Python lists, JSON strings, or comma/space-separated strings.\n        \"\"\"\n        if isinstance(input_value, list):\n            nums = input_value\n        elif isinstance(input_value, str):\n            try:\n                nums = parse_number_list_arg(input_value)\n            except ValueError as e:\n                raise ValidationError(f\"Invalid number list string: {e}\")\n        else:\n            raise ValidationError(\"Unsupported input type; must be a list or string.\")\n\n        if not nums:\n            return [] # Empty list is valid, handled by core logic as False\n\n        # Check if all elements are integers\n        if not all(isinstance(x, int) for x in nums):\n            raise ValidationError(\"All elements in the list must be integers.\")\n        \n        # Consider adding a max list size constraint here (e.g., if len(nums) > 100_000: ...)\n        return nums\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def parse_from_string(arg: str) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Parses a single argument string into a list of integers.\n        This method is a wrapper, delegating to the utility function and handling errors.\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            return parse_number_list_arg(arg)\n        except ValueError as e:\n            raise ValidationError(f\"Failed to parse numbers from string: {e}\")\n```\n\n```python\n# src/pairsum/service.py\nfrom typing import List\nfrom datetime import datetime\nimport logging\nfrom .models import PairSumResult, QueryRecord\nfrom .repository import PairSumRepository\nfrom .exceptions import PersistenceError\nfrom .utils import to_json_safe\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\nclass PairSumService:\n    \"\"\"\n    Encapsulates the core business logic for finding pairs that sum to zero\n    and orchestrates persistence of query history.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, repo: PairSumRepository):\n        self._repo = repo\n\n    def pairs_sum_to_zero(self, numbers: List[int]) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Determines if any two distinct elements in the list sum to zero.\n        Achieves O(n) time complexity by using a set to track seen numbers.\n        \"\"\"\n        seen = set()\n        zero_count = 0\n        for n in numbers:\n            if n == 0:\n                zero_count += 1\n                if zero_count >= 2:\n                    return True\n            # Check if complement (-n) is already seen.\n            # This ensures distinct elements (as `n` is not yet `seen` when checking `-n`).\n            if -n in seen:\n                return True\n            seen.add(n) # Add current number to the set for future checks\n        return False\n\n    def compute_and_record(self, numbers: List[int]) -> PairSumResult:\n        \"\"\"\n        Computes the pair sum result and attempts to record the query history.\n        Persistence failures are logged and do not prevent returning the computation result.\n        \"\"\"\n        result_bool = self.pairs_sum_to_zero(numbers)\n        \n        # Prepare QueryRecord\n        record = QueryRecord(\n            timestamp=datetime.utcnow(),\n            input_json=to_json_safe(numbers),\n            result=result_bool\n        )\n\n        try:\n            self._repo.save_query(record)\n        except PersistenceError as e:\n            logger.error(f\"Failed to persist query for input {numbers}. Error: {e}\")\n        except Exception as e:\n            logger.exception(f\"Unexpected error during query persistence for input {numbers}.\")\n\n        return PairSumResult(result=result_bool, input=numbers)\n```\n\n```python\n# src/pairsum/repository.py\nimport sqlite3\nimport threading\nimport os\nimport logging\nfrom typing import List\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom .models import QueryRecord\nfrom .exceptions import PersistenceError\nfrom .utils import to_json_safe\nimport json\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\nclass PairSumRepository:\n    \"\"\"\n    Persistence adapter for storing and retrieving QueryRecord entries.\n    Uses SQLite as the default backend.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, db_url: str):\n        # db_url format: sqlite:///path/to/db.db\n        self.db_path = db_url.split(\":///\", 1)[-1]\n        self._lock = threading.Lock() # Protects concurrent SQLite access\n        self._ensure_db_directory()\n        self.ensure_schema()\n\n    def _ensure_db_directory(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Ensures the directory for the SQLite database file exists.\"\"\"\n        db_dir = os.path.dirname(self.db_path)\n        if db_dir and not os.path.exists(db_dir):\n            try:\n                os.makedirs(db_dir, exist_ok=True)\n                logger.info(f\"Created database directory: {db_dir}\")\n            except OSError as e:\n                logger.error(f\"Failed to create database directory {db_dir}: {e}\")\n                raise PersistenceError(f\"Cannot create DB directory: {e}\")\n\n    def ensure_schema(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Creates the 'query_record' table and necessary indexes if they do not exist.\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            with self._lock, sqlite3.connect(self.db_path) as conn:\n                conn.execute(\"\"\"\n                    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS query_record (\n                      id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n                      timestamp TEXT NOT NULL,\n                      input_json TEXT NOT NULL,\n                      result INTEGER NOT NULL\n                    );\n                \"\"\")\n                conn.execute(\"CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS ix_query_timestamp ON query_record(timestamp);\")\n                conn.commit()\n            logger.info(f\"Ensured schema for database: {self.db_path}\")\n        except sqlite3.Error as e:\n            logger.error(f\"Error ensuring database schema: {e}\")\n            raise PersistenceError(f\"Failed to ensure DB schema: {e}\")\n\n    def save_query(self, record: QueryRecord) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Inserts a new query record into the database.\n        Returns the ID of the newly created record.\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            with self._lock, sqlite3.connect(self.db_path) as conn:\n                cursor = conn.execute(\n                    \"INSERT INTO query_record (timestamp, input_json, result) VALUES (?, ?, ?);\",\n                    (record.timestamp.isoformat(), record.input_json, int(record.result)),\n                )\n                conn.commit()\n                record_id = cursor.lastrowid\n                logger.debug(f\"Saved query record with ID: {record_id}\")\n                return record_id\n        except sqlite3.Error as e:\n            logger.error(f\"Error saving query record: {e}\")\n            raise PersistenceError(f\"Failed to save query: {e}\")\n\n    def list_queries(self, limit: int = 100) -> List[QueryRecord]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieves a list of recent query records from the database.\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            with self._lock, sqlite3.connect(self.db_path) as conn:\n                cursor = conn.execute(\n                    \"SELECT id, timestamp, input_json, result FROM query_record ORDER BY timestamp DESC LIMIT ?;\",\n                    (limit,)\n                )\n                records = []\n                for row in cursor.fetchall():\n                    try:\n                        record = QueryRecord(\n                            id=row[0],\n                            timestamp=datetime.fromisoformat(row[1]),\n                            input_json=row[2],\n                            result=bool(row[3])\n                        )\n                        records.append(record)\n                    except (ValueError, TypeError, json.JSONDecodeError) as e:\n                        logger.warning(f\"Could not parse query record ID {row[0]} from DB: {e}. Skipping record.\")\n                logger.debug(f\"Retrieved {len(records)} query records.\")\n                return records\n        except sqlite3.Error as e:\n            logger.error(f\"Error listing query records: {e}\")\n            raise PersistenceError(f\"Failed to list queries: {e}\")\n```\n\n```python\n# src/pairsum/controllers.py\nimport logging\nfrom typing import List\nfrom .service import PairSumService\nfrom .models import PairSumResult\nfrom .validators import PairSumValidator\nfrom .exceptions import ValidationError\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\nclass CLIController:\n    \"\"\"\n    Handles command-line interface interactions.\n    Orchestrates validation, service calls, and result output.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, service: PairSumService):\n        self._service = service\n\n    def run(self, input_arg: str) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Executes the CLI logic: parses input, computes result, prints, and returns exit code.\n        \"\"\"\n        try:\n            numbers = PairSumValidator.parse_from_string(input_arg)\n            if not numbers:\n                print(\"False\") # As per FR1: empty or single-element lists return False\n                return 0\n            \n            result = self._service.compute_and_record(numbers)\n            print(result.result)\n            return 0\n        except ValidationError as e:\n            logger.error(f\"CLI Input Error: {e.message}\")\n            print(f\"Error: {e.message}\")\n            return 2 # Exit code 2 for validation errors\n        except Exception as e:\n            logger.exception(\"An unexpected error occurred during CLI execution.\")\n            print(f\"An unexpected error occurred: {e}\")\n            return 1 # Generic error code\n\nclass LibraryController:\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides a simple programmatic interface for the core utility.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, service: PairSumService):\n        self._service = service\n\n    def check(self, numbers: List[int]) -> PairSumResult:\n        \"\"\"\n        Checks if the given list of integers contains a pair that sums to zero.\n        Performs validation and then calls the service layer.\n        \"\"\"\n        # The design specification says \"validate input -> compute result\".\n        # For the library API, the input type hint (List[int]) helps,\n        # but a robust check against non-int elements might be useful if not\n        # strictly guaranteed by the caller (which the prompt says to trust).\n        # Sticking to the design, PairSumValidator.validate_numbers can still be used.\n        valid_numbers = PairSumValidator.validate_numbers(numbers)\n        if not valid_numbers: # Empty or single-element lists will result in False by algorithm\n             return PairSumResult(result=False, input=valid_numbers)\n        return self._service.compute_and_record(valid_numbers)\n```\n\n```python\n# src/pairsum/api.py\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, status\nfrom fastapi.responses import JSONResponse\nfrom typing import Optional, List\nimport logging\n\nfrom .service import PairSumService\nfrom .validators import PairSumValidator\nfrom .models import PairSumRequest, PairSumResult, QueryRecord\nfrom .exceptions import ValidationError, PersistenceError\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\ndef create_app(service: PairSumService) -> FastAPI:\n    \"\"\"\n    Creates and configures the FastAPI application instance.\n    \"\"\"\n    app = FastAPI(\n        title=\"Pair Sum Zero Utility\",\n        description=\"API to check if a list of integers contains two distinct elements that sum to zero.\",\n        version=\"1.0.0\",\n    )\n\n    @app.exception_handler(ValidationError)\n    async def validation_exception_handler(request, exc: ValidationError):\n        \"\"\"Handles ValidationError and returns a 400 Bad Request.\"\"\"\n        logger.warning(f\"Validation error for request {request.url}: {exc.message}\")\n        return JSONResponse(\n            status_code=status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST,\n            content={\"detail\": exc.message},\n        )\n    \n    @app.exception_handler(PersistenceError)\n    async def persistence_exception_handler(request, exc: PersistenceError):\n        \"\"\"Handles PersistenceError and returns a 500 Internal Server Error.\"\"\"\n        logger.error(f\"Persistence error for request {request.url}: {exc.message}\")\n        return JSONResponse(\n            status_code=status.HTTP_500_INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR,\n            content={\"detail\": \"A database error occurred.\"},\n        )\n\n\n    @app.post(\"/pairsum\", response_model=PairSumResult)\n    async def post_pairsum(request: PairSumRequest):\n        \"\"\"\n        Checks if a pair of distinct integers sums to zero in the provided list.\n        \"\"\"\n        logger.info(f\"POST /pairsum received with numbers: {request.numbers}\")\n        # Validator will implicitly handle empty/single element lists by returning them\n        # The service's pairs_sum_to_zero will return False for these cases.\n        valid_numbers = PairSumValidator.validate_numbers(request.numbers) # Ensure contents are int\n        result = service.compute_and_record(valid_numbers)\n        return result\n\n    @app.get(\"/pairsum\", response_model=PairSumResult)\n    async def get_pairsum(numbers: str):\n        \"\"\"\n        Checks if a pair of distinct integers sums to zero, provided as a query parameter.\n        E.g., /pairsum?numbers=1,2,-1 or /pairsum?numbers=[1,2,-1]\n        \"\"\"\n        logger.info(f\"GET /pairsum received with numbers string: {numbers}\")\n        valid_numbers = PairSumValidator.parse_from_string(numbers)\n        result = service.compute_and_record(valid_numbers)\n        return result\n\n    @app.get(\"/queries\", response_model=List[QueryRecord])\n    async def get_queries(limit: int = 100):\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieves a list of historical query records. Admin endpoint.\n        \"\"\"\n        logger.info(f\"GET /queries received with limit: {limit}\")\n        try:\n            queries = service._repo.list_queries(limit=limit)\n            return queries\n        except PersistenceError as e:\n            raise HTTPException(status_code=status.HTTP_500_INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR, detail=\"Failed to retrieve query history.\") from e\n        \n    return app\n```\n\n```python\n# src/pairsum/cli.py\nimport sys\nimport argparse\nimport logging\n\nfrom .config import Config\nfrom .repository import PairSumRepository\nfrom .service import PairSumService\nfrom .controllers import CLIController\nfrom .exceptions import ValidationError\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\ndef main(argv=None) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Main entry point for the command-line interface.\n    Parses arguments, initializes components, and runs the CLI controller.\n    \"\"\"\n    if argv is None:\n        argv = sys.argv[1:]\n\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=\"Check if a list of integers contains two distinct elements that sum to zero.\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"numbers\",\n        type=str,\n        help=\"A list of integers, e.g., '1,2,-1' or '[1,2,-1]' or '1 2 -1'.\"\n    )\n\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n\n    # Initialize dependencies\n    config = Config()\n    repository = PairSumRepository(config.db_url)\n    service = PairSumService(repository)\n    cli_controller = CLIController(service)\n\n    # Run the controller logic\n    exit_code = cli_controller.run(args.numbers)\n    return exit_code\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n```\n\n```python\n# src/pairsum/db/migrations/0001_create_queryrecord.sql\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS query_record (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n  timestamp TEXT NOT NULL,\n  input_json TEXT NOT NULL,\n  result INTEGER NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS ix_query_timestamp ON query_record(timestamp);\n```\n\n```python\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"pairsum\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Utility to find if two distinct integers in a list sum to zero.\"\nauthors = [\n    { name = \"Your Name\", email = \"your.email@example.com\" },\n]\ndependencies = [\n    \"fastapi>=0.111.0\",\n    \"uvicorn[standard]>=0.29.0\",\n    \"pydantic>=2.0.0\",\n]\nrequires-python = \">=3.9\"\n\n[project.scripts]\npairsum = \"pairsum.cli:main\"\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"setuptools>=61.0\", \"wheel\"]\nbuild-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\"\n\n[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\nwhere = [\"src\"]\n\n[tool.pytest.ini_options]\npythonpath = [\"src\"]\n```\n\n```python\n# README.md\n# Pair Sum Zero Utility\n\nThis project provides a utility to determine if a list of integers contains two distinct elements that sum to zero. It exposes programmatic, command-line (CLI), and HTTP (REST) interfaces.\n\n## Features\n\n- **Core Logic**: Efficient O(n) algorithm to find if `a + b = 0` for `a != b` in a list.\n- **Programmatic API**: A Python library function for direct integration.\n- **CLI Tool**: A command-line interface for interactive use or scripting.\n- **HTTP API**: A RESTful API (FastAPI) for network access, returning JSON responses.\n- **Query History**: Persistence of query inputs and results to an SQLite database for auditing and analytics.\n- **Robust Validation**: Comprehensive input validation across all interfaces.\n\n## Installation\n\n1.  **Clone the repository:**\n    ```bash\n    git clone https://github.com/your-username/pairsum-utility.git\n    cd pairsum-utility\n    ```\n\n2.  **Create a virtual environment and install dependencies:**\n    ```bash\n    python -m venv venv\n    source venv/bin/activate  # On Windows: venv\\Scripts\\activate\n    pip install --upgrade pip\n    pip install -e .\n    ```\n\n## Usage\n\n### 1. Command-Line Interface (CLI)\n\nThe CLI tool `pairsum` can be run with a list of numbers:\n\n```bash\npairsum \"1,2,-1\"\n# Output: True\n\npairsum \"[1, 3, 5, 0]\"\n# Output: False\n\npairsum \"2 4 -5 3 5 7\"\n# Output: True\n\npairsum \"10\"\n# Output: False\n\npairsum \"0 0\"\n# Output: True\n\npairsum \"invalid input\"\n# Output: Error: Failed to parse numbers from string: Invalid number format in string: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'invalid'\n# Exit code: 2\n```\n\n### 2. Programmatic Python API\n\nYou can import and use the utility in your Python code:\n\n```python\nfrom pairsum import check_pair_sum_to_zero\n\n# Example 1\nresult = check_pair_sum_to_zero([1, 3, 5, 0])\nprint(f\"Result for [1, 3, 5, 0]: {result.result}\")\n# Output: Result for [1, 3, 5, 0]: False\n\n# Example 2\nresult = check_pair_sum_to_zero([2, 4, -5, 3, 5, 7])\nprint(f\"Result for [2, 4, -5, 3, 5, 7]: {result.result}\")\n# Output: Result for [2, 4, -5, 3, 5, 7]: True\n\n# Access input from result object\nprint(f\"Input for Example 2: {result.input}\")\n\n# Example 3: Error handling\nfrom pairsum.exceptions import ValidationError\ntry:\n    check_pair_sum_to_zero([1, 'a', 3]) # This will raise ValidationError\nexcept ValidationError as e:\n    print(f\"Programmatic Error: {e.message}\")\n```\n\n### 3. HTTP API (RESTful)\n\nStart the API server using `uvicorn`:\n\n```bash\nuvicorn pairsum.api:create_app --factory --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000\n```\n\nThe `--factory` flag tells uvicorn to call `create_app` without arguments to get the FastAPI application.\n\nThe API provides the following endpoints:\n\n#### `POST /pairsum`\n\n-   **Description**: Checks for a pair summing to zero in a list provided in the request body.\n-   **Method**: `POST`\n-   **URL**: `http://localhost:8000/pairsum`\n-   **Request Body (JSON)**:\n    ```json\n    {\n        \"numbers\": [2, 4, -5, 3, 5, 7]\n    }\n    ```\n-   **Response (JSON - 200 OK)**:\n    ```json\n    {\n        \"result\": true,\n        \"input\": [2, 4, -5, 3, 5, 7]\n    }\n    ```\n-   **Error Response (JSON - 400 Bad Request)**:\n    ```json\n    {\n        \"detail\": \"All elements in the list must be integers.\"\n    }\n    ```\n\n#### `GET /pairsum`\n\n-   **Description**: Checks for a pair summing to zero, with numbers provided as a query parameter.\n-   **Method**: `GET`\n-   **URL**: `http://localhost:8000/pairsum?numbers=1,2,-1` or `http://localhost:8000/pairsum?numbers=[1,2,-1]`\n-   **Response (JSON - 200 OK)**:\n    ```json\n    {\n        \"result\": true,\n        \"input\": [1, 2, -1]\n    }\n    ```\n\n#### `GET /queries`\n\n-   **Description**: Retrieves a list of historical query records. (Admin endpoint)\n-   **Method**: `GET`\n-   **URL**: `http://localhost:8000/queries?limit=5`\n-   **Response (JSON - 200 OK)**:\n    ```json\n    [\n        {\n            \"id\": 1,\n            \"timestamp\": \"2023-10-27T10:00:00.123456\",\n            \"input_json\": \"[1,2,-1]\",\n            \"result\": true\n        }\n        // ... more records\n    ]\n    ```\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 src/\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 pairsum/\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 __init__.py         # Package initializer, exports public API & init logging\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 config.py           # Configuration settings\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 models.py           # Data Transfer Objects (DTOs) and ORM models\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 exceptions.py       # Custom exception classes\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 validators.py       # Input parsing & validation logic\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 service.py          # Core business logic and orchestration\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 repository.py       # Persistence adapter (SQLite)\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 controllers.py      # CLI and Library API controllers\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 api.py              # FastAPI application factory and endpoints\n\u2502       \u251c\u2500\u2500 cli.py              # CLI entrypoint\n\u2502       \u2514\u2500\u2500 utils.py            # General utility functions\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 data/\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 pairsum.db              # (Runtime) Default SQLite database file\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 pyproject.toml              # Project metadata and dependencies\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 README.md                   # This README file\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 .gitignore\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\nThe application can be configured using environment variables:\n\n-   `PAIRSUM_DB_URL`: The database connection string (default: `sqlite:///data/pairsum.db`).\n-   `PAIRSUM_LOG_LEVEL`: Logging level (e.g., `INFO`, `DEBUG`, `WARNING`, `ERROR`; default: `INFO`).\n\nFor example, to change the database path:\n`PAIRSUM_DB_URL=\"sqlite:///./my_custom_path/history.db\" uvicorn pairsum.api:create_app --factory`\n\n## Development\n\n### Running Tests (Not provided in generation, but for project development)\n\n```bash\npytest\n```\n\n### Linting & Formatting\n\n(Not included in this generation, but tools like `flake8` and `black` are recommended)\n\n```\n# black .\n# flake8 src/\n```\n\n## Contributing\n\n(Standard contributing guidelines would go here)\n\n## License\n\n(Standard license information would go here)\n```\n\n```python\n# .gitignore\n# Byte-code files\n*.pyc\n__pycache__/\n\n# Distribution / packaging\n.Python\nbuild/\ndist/\n*.egg-info/\n.env/\nvenv/\n\n# IDE files\n.vscode/\n.idea/\n\n# OS generated files\n.DS_Store\n.Trashes\nThumbs.db\n\n# Data